+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+See proposal.md for motivation. The platform currently models carts and order products through catalog-product relationships, while order data already has purchase-time quantity and price fields. Product deletion must sever live-catalog dependencies without deleting or invalidating transactional records.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+
+- Make cart reads, mutation, and checkout resilient to missing catalog products.
+- Make order mapping independent of a currently live product and preserve purchase-time fields.
+- Keep deleted SKUs unavailable through public product lookup.
+- Cover the behavior with focused service/API regression tests.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+
+- Changing product deletion authorization, catalog search semantics for live products, or order pricing rules for newly created orders.
+- Restoring deleted products or introducing a product recycle bin.
+
+## Decisions
+
+1. Treat catalog presence as the source of truth for active-cart eligibility. Cart hydration and calculations filter missing products before producing the readable cart, and cart mutation/checkout paths use the same eligibility check. This keeps deleted items from being silently reintroduced; retaining orphaned cart rows without filtering would keep breaking reads.
+
+2. Make order-product presentation use persisted order snapshot data rather than dereferencing the catalog product. Where the existing order-product schema lacks a snapshot field, populate and persist the required SKU/name/price values at order creation and map them directly for all order endpoints. This is preferred over soft-deleting catalog products because public SKU lookup must still return 404 and unrelated catalog operations must remain unchanged.
+
+3. Keep deletion itself focused on catalog records and dependent live-cart cleanup/compatibility. Do not cascade-delete order products, order totals, or order status history.
+
+4. Add regression coverage at service and integration/API boundaries, including mixed and empty carts, add rejection, checkout, all specified order reads/status update, and v2 SKU lookup.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Existing historical rows may lack one or more snapshot fields] → Use the order-product values already persisted by the purchase flow and add a migration/default mapping only where required; tests must cover the deletion sequence.
+- [Different API versions may map orders through separate DTO paths] → Exercise both private and authenticated single/list endpoints and centralize snapshot mapping where possible.
+- [Orphaned cart rows may remain persisted] → Filter them consistently and remove them during cart updates/checkout so subsequent reads and calculations remain stable.
+
+## Migration Plan
+
+Deploy the code and any additive schema migration together. Existing orders remain readable from their persisted order-product data; rollback restores the previous code while leaving additive snapshot data intact. No destructive data migration is required.
